  clock: 1  # 5f0d
  nonce: 5f0d  # 5f0d
  data:  # 5f0d
    kind: invariant  # 5f0d
    title: "JS/TS analyzer populates call_construct on ZERO call edges (measured 4,872 across 3 repos), making the io-boundary F3 method gate unconditional for the second-largest sink surface"  # 5f0d
    status: violated  # 5f0d
    priority: 1  # 5f0d
    parent:  # 5f0d
    tags:  # 5f0d
    - taint  # 5f0d
    - io_boundary  # 5f0d
    - javascript  # 5f0d
    isbefore: []  # 5f0d
    duplicate_of: []  # 5f0d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5f0d
    pr_ref:  # 5f0d
    description: "MEASURED ON THREE REAL REPOS. The JS/TS analyzer populates meta.call_construct on\nZERO call edges, while other analyzers populate it richly:\n\n  repo            language     call edges   with call_construct\n  alertmanager    go               10,172   7,741 (6,012 `method`)\n  alertmanager    javascript           28   0\n  alertmanager    typescript           77   0\n  apollo-server   javascript          169   0\n  apollo-server   typescript        2,828   0\n  sherpa-onnx     javascript        1,749   0\n  sherpa-onnx     typescript           26   0\n  sherpa-onnx     python            9,859   5,138\n  sherpa-onnx     kotlin            2,414   0\n\nCONSEQUENCE. io-boundary:F3 gates the no-module-hint path on call_construct: a\nmethod-kind catalogue entry is refused unless the edge is a method-construct call.\nWith the field never populated, F3's method branch is UNCONDITIONAL for JS/TS,\nexactly as INV-funit found it to be for the sanitizer gate before PR #-. JS carries\n50 catalogued sources and 83 sinks -- the largest sink surface of any language after\npython -- so this is not a corner.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, stated so nobody closes this on the strength of the table:\nI have NOT measured whether JS io-boundary tags actually drop as a result. The\nsurvey map does not carry io_boundary meta (it is stamped downstream), so the\nobvious check is unavailable from these artifacts and I did not substitute a\nproxy. The next step is a direct io-boundaries run on apollo-server comparing\ntagged counts against the catalogue's method-kind vs function-kind entries.\n\nHOW IT SURFACED. The verify-claims method-starvation coverage gate keyed on\ncall_construct and reported `fs`, `console` and `process` starved on BOTH real JS\nrepos -- i.e. it would have downgraded every JavaScript repo. The gate now ABSTAINS\nfor any language that never stamps the field (None = could not check, not empty =\nchecked and found none), which preserves today's behaviour and converts this from a\nsilent hole into a filed one. When this item is fixed, that abstention should be\nrevisited: JS becomes checkable and the gate gets stricter for free."  # 5f0d
    fields:  # 5f0d
      statement: "Every analyzer that emits call edges consumed by the io-boundary F3 gate must populate meta.call_construct, or the gate's method branch is unconditional for that language."  # 5f0d
      root_cause: "The JS/TS analyzer never sets meta.call_construct. F3 refuses a method-kind catalogue entry unless the edge carries call_construct=='method', so for JS/TS that refusal can never be lifted."  # 5f0d
- op: discuss  # e12a
  at: '2026-08-11T17:51:28Z'  # e12a
  by: agent  # e12a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e12a
  clock: 2  # e12a
  nonce: e12a  # e12a
  message: "PREMISE PARTLY CHECKED AND THE STATED CONSEQUENCE IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED -- plus a\nDIFFERENT JS recall gap turned up while checking it.\n\n1) THE FP CHANNEL THIS ITEM IMPLIES IS NOT REACHABLE ON THE OBVIOUS SHAPE. With\ncall_construct absent, gate_named_entry's `if call_construct == \"method\": return None`\nrefusal never fires for JS, so a method call on an untyped receiver can in principle\nmatch a FUNCTION-kind catalogue entry by bare name. Probed directly:\n\n    items.join(',')      // Array.prototype.join   vs catalogued path.join   (fs_read)\n    s.normalize()        // String.prototype       vs path.normalize\n    p.resolve()          //                        vs path.resolve\n    fs.readFileSync(...) // positive control\n\nResult: ONE boundary -- the control. The three method calls on untyped receivers produce\nno boundary at all, because the analyzer emits no edge for them in the first place. So on\nthis shape the gate is not reached and there is nothing for it to refuse.\n\n2) INDEPENDENTLY, measurement 0001 adjudicated every JavaScript taint flow in a 6-repo\npopulation: 4 adjudicable flows, precision 0%, and every one of them was\n`co-location-file` or an unanchored `<external>` source -- NOT an F3 bare-name match. So\nno JS false positive in that population came through this channel either.\n\n3) WHAT DID TURN UP, from measurement 0002: a `require` INSIDE a function is 97.9% blind.\nModule-scope `const m = require('fs'); ... m.readFile(p)` attributes 182 of 187\ncatalogued primitives; the identical call with the `require` moved into the function body\nattributes 4. Lazy `require` inside a function is ordinary JS (optional dependencies,\nstartup cost). That is a real recall gap with a clear shape, and it is not what this item\ndescribes.\n\nSTATUS UNCHANGED: violated. The table of zeros in the filing is accurate -- JS/TS really\ndoes populate call_construct on no call edge. What is still unmeasured is the item's own\nstated next step: a direct io-boundaries run on apollo-server comparing tagged counts\nagainst method-kind vs function-kind entries. Neither of the two probes above substitutes\nfor it, and neither should be read as closing this."  # e12a
